    </div>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <input type="checkbox" id="sv-toggle" class="sv-toggle sr-only" />
      <p class="sv-text" style="margin:0">
        <strong>Booth briefing.</strong> An analogue mixer whose own paperwork
        disagrees: the product page says {XONE_92.channelsProductPage} channels,
        the technical specification sheet says {XONE_92.channelsSpecSheet}. Its
        {XONE_92.filters} stereo filters, one either side of the crossfader, are
        why it exists. There is no firmware, no drivers and no soundcard, so
        nothing to update and nothing that can be out of date. Before you touch
        it: the mics reach neither Booth nor Record until a switch on the rear
        panel says so, and the master is unity {XONE_92.masterUnityPosition},
        not fully clockwise.
      </p>
      <label for="sv-toggle" class="sv-more">Read more…</label>
      <label for="sv-toggle" class="sv-more sv-more-open">Read less</label>
    </div>

    <AccGroup icon="zap" title="Emergency fixes" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="mic-not-in-record"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The mic is loud out front, absent from the booth and absent from the recording</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> That is the mixer working as designed.
        Allen &amp; Heath route the mic inputs through a clean feed circuit, and
        their wording is that by default MIC 1 and 2 do not route to the BOOTH or
        RECORD outputs. The switches that change it are on the rear panel.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A clean feed exists so an MC or a
        compere does not end up in the recording and does not feed back into the
        booth wedge. The side effect in a live room is exactly what it sounds
        like: the MC is loud in the room, the DJ cannot hear them in the booth,
        and the set recording comes back with the music and none of the voice.
        Nothing on the top panel tells you this is happening.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Go behind the mixer. The mic clean feed
        switches are on the rear panel, listed by Allen &amp; Heath as a Mk2
        feature, so reach them before the desk is boxed into a booth. If the mic
        must be in the recording, that switch is the only thing that puts it
        there. If the desk is a Mk1, or the switches are not reachable, take the
        mic into a spare music channel instead, which does feed Booth and
        Record like any other source.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Decide before the doors open
        whether the mic belongs in the booth and in the recording, then set the
        rear panel to match. It is a two minute job with the desk out and an
        impossible one with the desk in.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#rear-panel-first">Walk the rear panel first</a> <a href={XONE_92.sourceMicRouting} rel="noopener">Allen &amp; Heath on mic signals and the record output</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="crossfader-does-nothing"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The crossfader does nothing</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Look at the crossfader assign switch on
        that channel. It has three positions and the middle one takes the channel
        out of the crossfader entirely. Allen &amp; Heath put it plainly: in the
        central position the music signal level is unaffected by the
        crossfader.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> On the mixers most DJs learn on, a
        centre or THRU position means "always on, ignore the crossfader", and
        that is what it means here too. The trap is that it is the same switch
        you use to assign a channel to side A or side B, so a previous DJ who
        wanted a channel permanently live has left it centred, and the next
        person sweeps the crossfader and hears nothing change.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Set the assign switch to the side you
        want the channel on, A or B, and check the other three channels while you
        are there. Then confirm the crossfader is actually doing something on a
        channel you can hear.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> The crossfader assign row is part
        of the same pre set pass as gain and filter assign, on all four music
        channels, not just the two you plan to use.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#returns-and-crossfader">A return will not crossfade</a> <a href="#crossfader-part">The crossfader is a replaceable part</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="filter-does-nothing"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">A filter does nothing on a channel</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The filter assign switch has a centre
        position too, and it is dry. Allen &amp; Heath's wording: in the central
        position the music signal is not affected by the filter.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> Either filter can be assigned to
        any of the four stereo channels, so the assign switch decides whether the
        channel goes to filter 1, to filter 2, or to neither. Centre is neither.
        A DJ who dials frequency and resonance hard while the channel sits in the
        centre position hears a filter that appears to be broken, and the LED
        state on the filter itself gives no clue, because the filter is running
        fine and simply has nothing routed into it.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Move the channel's filter assign switch
        to the filter you are actually holding, then check the filter's own
        ON switch and its type selection, HPF, BPF or LPF. If it still does
        nothing on every channel, read the next section before you call it a
        fault.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Set filter assign per channel as
        part of the pre set pass, and know which of the two filters is yours
        before the transition, not during it.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#filters">The two filters, explained</a> <a href="#filters-disabled">The filters do nothing at all</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="filters-disabled"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The filters do nothing at all, or will not scream</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> This desk can be modified. The internal
        options Allen &amp; Heath document include Filter Disable and a Maximum
        Resonance Limit, so a Xone:92 that will not filter, or will not go wild,
        may have been set up that way on purpose rather than broken.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> These are installer options, and
        they exist for venues: an install engineer who has heard one too many
        resonant sweeps at full tilt through an expensive system can cap the
        resonance, or take the filters out of the signal path altogether. Nothing
        on the top panel indicates it. The controls still move, the LEDs still
        light, and the sound does not change the way you expect.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Rule out the ordinary causes first, the
        channel's filter assign switch centred and the filter ON switch off. If
        every channel behaves the same way on both filters, treat it as a
        configured desk and ask the venue's engineer rather than working the
        controls harder. It is not a setting you can reach from the front.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> If a venue's Xone is filtered
        down, you know for next time, and a filter led set built on a resonant
        sweep needs a plan B in that room.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#filter-does-nothing">A filter does nothing on a channel</a> <a href="#filters">The two filters, explained</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="master-level"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The house system is clipping, or the master seems short of level</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The master control is not unity at
        maximum. Allen &amp; Heath put unity, 0dBu, {XONE_92.masterUnityPosition}.
        Fully clockwise gives {XONE_92.masterAtMax}.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A master knob that keeps going past
        unity is normal on a professional desk and invisible to a DJ who has been
        taught that maximum is the reference. Pinned fully clockwise into a house
        system that is already set for unity, the extra output has to go
        somewhere, and it goes into the system's input stage.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Bring the master back to
        {XONE_92.masterUnityPosition}, then set the room's level from the house
        system, not from the mixer. Use the channel meters, -20dBu to +10dBu,
        to see where the signal actually sits rather than judging by the knob.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Set master to unity at soundcheck
        and leave it there for the night. Loudness is the engineer's control, not
        yours.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href={XONE_92.sourceMasterLevels} rel="noopener">Allen &amp; Heath on Xone master level settings</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="returns-and-crossfader"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">A return will not crossfade</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> It never will. The return inputs do not
        pass through the crossfader. Allen &amp; Heath describe them as sources
        which do not need to route through the crossfader, which is a design
        decision rather than a limitation you can switch off.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> The returns share their channels
        with the two mic inputs, as MIC / RTN 1 and 2, and those channels exist
        for effects units, a second room feed, an announcement source and
        anything else that should stay live regardless of where the crossfader
        is.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> If the source has to be crossfaded,
        take it into one of the four stereo music channels on line instead, and
        assign that channel to a crossfader side. Leave the returns for what they
        are good at.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Plan the patch around it. Any
        source you intend to cut with the crossfader belongs on a music channel
        from the start.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#crossfader-does-nothing">The crossfader does nothing</a> <a href="#channels">Channel count, and two official answers</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="phono-shorting-plugs"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Noise on an unused phono input, and the plug in the wrong socket</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Unused phono inputs want shorting plugs
        in them, Allen &amp; Heath part {XONE_92.phonoShortingPlugPart}. When you
        pull one out to connect a turntable, put it somewhere safe. Their own
        warning: if removing shorting plugs to connect turntables, do not insert
        them into other RCA inputs or outputs, this results in very poor
        audio.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A phono input is a high gain stage
        expecting a cartridge, and an open one is an aerial. A shorting plug ties
        it quiet. The second half of the warning is the one that costs a set: a
        spare plug parked in a line input or, worse, an output, shorts a working
        signal, and the symptom reads as a dead channel or a ruined mix rather
        than as a plug in the wrong hole.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Count the shorting plugs against the
        phono inputs you are not using, and check that none of them has ended up
        in a line input, the record output or the mix outputs. This is documented
        as general guidance across the Xone series rather than as a Xone:92
        specific note, so it applies to whichever Xone you are standing at.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Keep the pulled plugs in your bag
        or taped to the rear of the desk, never in another socket.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#rear-panel-first">Walk the rear panel first</a> <a href="#hum-and-ground-loops">Hum and ground loops</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="shield" title="Safety" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="mains-earth"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Never lift the mains earth</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Do not remove the earth from the mixer's
        mains plug, and do not accept a lifted earth as somebody else's fix for
        hum. Allen &amp; Heath's own wording, quoted in full because paraphrasing
        it would soften it: "because the Xone mixer has a metal chassis, not
        plastic, it is essential that the mixer be grounded via the mains cable.
        Under no circumstances should you consider removing the green and yellow
        mains earth wire from the mains plug to the Xone, as if you do so, then
        should a fault develop in the mixer's power supply there is a risk that
        the chassis of the mixer could become live; a potentially lethal
        fault."</p>
        <p><strong>Why this matters.</strong> Lifting the earth is a common booth
        habit, because it does often silence a ground loop, and it works by
        removing the protection that keeps the metal you are touching at a safe
        potential. The mixer sits between two hands, a microphone and a
        turntable. This is the one item on this page that is not about a
        set going wrong.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> If you find a Xone on a lifted earth, a cut
        earth pin or a two pin adaptor, stop and tell the venue. Fix hum in the
        audio cabling instead, which is what Allen &amp; Heath tell you to do and
        what the next section sets out.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#hum-and-ground-loops">Hum and ground loops, the published procedure</a> <a href={XONE_92.sourceEarth} rel="noopener">Allen &amp; Heath on laptop hum through a Xone mixer</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="hum-and-ground-loops"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Hum and ground loops, the published procedure</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Confirm it is a ground loop by running
        the laptop on battery with its power supply unplugged. If the hum goes,
        it is a loop, and the cure belongs in the audio cabling.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A loop is a second path to earth
        between two devices that are each already earthed. The mixer's earth is
        not the problem to remove, it is the protection to keep.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Allen &amp; Heath's procedure, in their
        order: test on battery to confirm the loop, keep unbalanced cables short,
        do not coil excess cable, they suggest a figure of eight system to avoid
        turning the cable into a transformer, keep audio and mains cabling apart
        and cross them at right angles, and if the hum survives all of that,
        isolate the ground in the audio cabling or fit a ground loop isolator.
        Turntables earth to the chassis earth terminal on the rear of the mixer,
        and the Mk2 ships a dual ground adaptor so two decks can be earthed
        independently.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Carry a ground loop isolator and a
        short unbalanced cable set. Both are cheaper than the compromise
        somebody will otherwise suggest with a pair of pliers.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#mains-earth">Never lift the mains earth</a> <a href="#phono-shorting-plugs">Noise on an unused phono input</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="warn" title="Known issues" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="no-firmware"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">There is no firmware, and what that means both ways</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Allen &amp; Heath publish no firmware,
        no drivers and no release notes for this mixer, and there is no USB audio
        to need any. The only digital connector on it is
        {XONE_92.digitalIo}. So there is no version number on this mixer to
        check, and nothing on it that can be out of date. Checked
        {UPDATED}.</p>
        <p><strong>What that buys you.</strong> There is nothing to update before
        a gig, nothing that can be out of date, no release note to read, no
        installer to run, and no behaviour that quietly changes between one
        venue's desk and the next. A Xone:92 you learned on in 2010 behaves the
        same way tonight.</p>
        <p><strong>What it costs you.</strong> Nothing can be fixed in software
        either. If a routing decision on this mixer is not the one you would have
        made, that is the mixer, permanently: the mics stay out of Record until
        the rear panel switch is thrown, the assign centre positions stay as they
        are, and no update is coming to change any of it. Allen &amp; Heath
        describe the signal path as {XONE_92.analogueClaim}, and that cuts both
        ways.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Spend the time you would have spent checking
        a version on the rear panel and the assign switches instead. That is
        where this desk's surprises live.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#no-troubleshooting-section">The user guide has no troubleshooting section</a> <a href="#mic-not-in-record">The mic is absent from the recording</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="no-troubleshooting-section"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The user guide has no troubleshooting section</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> It does not have one. The Xone:92 user
        guide documents controls, connections and specifications, and there is no
        troubleshooting chapter, no symptom list and no fault table anywhere in
        it. A DJ holding the manual in a booth with a problem has nothing to work
        from.</p>
        <p><strong>Why we are saying it plainly.</strong> That is a checkable
        statement about a document rather than a complaint about a mixer, and it
        is most of why this page exists. The behaviours a DJ reads as faults on
        this desk, the mic missing from the recording, the dead crossfader, the
        dry filter, are all documented in the guide, just as descriptions of
        controls rather than as answers to symptoms. Nobody reading a manual
        under pressure finds them that way.</p>
        <p><strong>What is published, and what is not.</strong> Allen &amp; Heath
        publish no service manual for this mixer. They do publish a block
        diagram, and for a technician or a curious owner that is the closest
        thing to service documentation available: it shows where every stage sits
        in the signal path, including which points the crossfader and the filters
        are inserted at.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Keep the user guide for connector pinouts and
        control names, and use the emergency sections above for symptoms. If you
        hit a repeatable behaviour that is not covered here,
        <a href="/feedback">tell us what happened</a> and we will verify it
        against Allen &amp; Heath's own documents before it goes on the page.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">
          <strong>Official documents.</strong> <a href={XONE_92.sourceManual} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mk2 user guide (PDF) →</a><br />
          <a href={XONE_92.sourceSpecs} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mk2 technical specification (PDF) →</a><br />
          <a href={XONE_92.sourceBlockDiagram} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mk2 block diagram (PDF) →</a><br />
          <span class="dim" style="font-size:0.85rem">Broken link? <a href="/feedback">Let us know</a> and we'll fix it.</span>
        </p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#no-firmware">There is no firmware</a> <a href={XONE_92.source} rel="noopener">Allen &amp; Heath Xone:92 product page</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="sliders" title="Features explained" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="filters"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The two filters, and why the mixer exists</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Allen &amp; Heath's own description:
        {XONE_92.filters} stereo VCFs are provided, one either side of the
        crossfader. Each is switchable between {XONE_92.filterTypes.join(', ')},
        has an ON and OFF switch, and carries its own frequency sweep and
        resonance. Either filter can be assigned to any of the four stereo
        channels.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> This is the control the mixer is
        bought for. Each filter carries {XONE_92.filterResonance}, and at the wild end
        Allen &amp; Heath describe the result as resonant feedback boost, which is
        the sound people mean when they say a Xone filter. Because there are two
        of them and they sit either side of the crossfader, you can filter what
        is leaving and what is arriving as separate moves during one
        transition.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> Sweeping the outgoing track
        out through a high pass while the incoming one opens up underneath it, or
        holding a resonant band on a loop to build a breakdown without an effects
        unit anywhere in the chain.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Leaving a channel's filter assign in
        the centre position and blaming the filter, and pushing resonance to the
        wild end on a large system, where the feedback boost is far louder than it
        sounds on headphones. Allen &amp; Heath do not publish a frequency range,
        a slope or a resonance figure for this filter, so the only way to know
        where it bites is to sweep it before the room is full.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#lfos">The two LFOs</a> <a href="#filter-does-nothing">A filter does nothing on a channel</a> <a href="#filters-disabled">The filters do nothing at all</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="lfos"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The two LFOs</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> {XONE_92.lfos} independent low frequency
        oscillators modulate the filters, with tap tempo and an x2 speed
        setting.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> An LFO moves a filter for you, in
        time, so a sweep can run under a track while both your hands are
        elsewhere. Tap tempo is what makes it musical on an analogue desk with no
        clock of its own coming in: you give it the tempo, it holds it.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> A slow pulse on a long
        blend, or the x2 setting to double the rate into a build without
        re tapping.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Tapping the tempo once and trusting
        it across a tempo change, and leaving an LFO running on a channel after
        the transition, which reads to the room as a mix that will not settle.
        LFO 2's tap tempo also sends MIDI clock with start and stop, so a tap
        made for the filter can move anything you have slaved to the mixer.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#filters">The two filters</a> <a href="#midi">MIDI out</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="channels"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Channel count, and two official answers</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Both of these are Allen &amp; Heath's
        own numbers. Their product page calls it a
        {XONE_92.channelsProductPage} channel analogue mixer. Their technical
        specification sheet for the same mixer states channels
        {XONE_92.channelsSpecSheet}. We publish both rather than choosing one,
        because both are official.</p>
        <p><strong>What is actually in front of you.</strong> The layout is
        {XONE_92.channelsDetail}. Count the two mic and return channels as music
        channels and you get one number, count them separately and you get the
        other, which is the likeliest reason two documents from the same company
        disagree.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> It decides what you can patch. Four
        stereo channels take turntables or line sources and go through the EQ,
        the filters and the crossfader. The mic and return channels, {XONE_92.micInputs} of them,
        take a microphone on XLR or a line return, and they do not pass through
        the crossfader. There are also {XONE_92.auxSends} aux sends, switchable pre
        or post fader and fed from all six channels.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Reading 6 + 2 and planning six
        turntables, or reading 4 + 2 and not realising the return channels are
        full channels with their own faders.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#returns-and-crossfader">A return will not crossfade</a> <a href="#mic-not-in-record">The mic is absent from the recording</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="midi"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">MIDI out</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> {XONE_92.digitalIo}, and that is the
        only digital connection on the mixer. It is assignable, sends CC and note
        on and off messages, and defaults to MIDI channel 16 with a switch to
        15.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> The mixer can drive software or
        hardware from its own controls without becoming a controller. The
        published assignments include FILTER 1 FREQ on CC12, FILTER 2 FREQ on
        CC13 and the crossfader on CC92, and LFO 2's tap tempo sends MIDI clocks
        along with start and stop.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> Sending the filter sweep or
        the crossfader position to a software instrument, or clocking an external
        machine from the tap tempo so the whole rig follows the same pulse.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Expecting MIDI in. There is one DIN
        and it is an output, so anything you want to control the mixer with has
        nowhere to plug in. Expecting audio over that connector as well: there is
        no soundcard on this mixer at all.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#lfos">The two LFOs</a> <a href="#compatibility">What it works with</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="headphones"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Headphones, cue and the meters</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Two headphone sockets, one full size TRS
        and one mini jack, with a cue and mix blend and a split cue mode. Allen
        &amp; Heath recommend closed ear headphones rated
        35 to 300 ohms.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Split cue puts the cued source in one
        ear and the mix in the other, which is how you beatmatch on a loud stage
        where the wedge is fighting you. The blend control is the more common
        cause of a headphone problem than the socket is: fully to one side and you
        are hearing only the master, which sounds like a cue that has stopped
        working.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> Split cue on any stage
        where the monitor is unreliable, and the second socket for a guest or a
        back to back handover without unplugging.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Judging level by ear alone. The
        meters are nine LED covering -20dBu to +10dBu, on the channels
        and on the master, and they are the reference to set gain against.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#master-level">The house system is clipping</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="bulb" title="Professional tips" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="rear-panel-first"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Walk the rear panel before the desk goes in the booth</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> On this mixer more of the routing than
        you expect is decided behind it, and once the desk is installed you
        cannot reach any of it.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> The mic clean feed switches that
        decide whether an MC reaches Booth and Record are on the rear panel. So
        are the phono and line inputs sharing sockets with shorting plugs in
        them, the chassis earth terminal every turntable needs, and the outputs
        that decide which system gets which feed: MIX 1 on balanced XLR, MIX 2 on unbalanced TRS, BOOTH on unbalanced TRS and RECORD on RCA.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Before the desk is boxed in, set the clean
        feed switches for the night, confirm the shorting plugs are in phono
        inputs and nowhere else, earth the turntables to the chassis terminal,
        and confirm the house system is coming from the output you think it
        is.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Discovering the clean feed switch
        exists during the set, when it is a hand's width from your fingers and a
        booth panel away.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#mic-not-in-record">The mic is absent from the recording</a> <a href="#phono-shorting-plugs">Noise on an unused phono input</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="shield" title="Preventive maintenance" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="crossfader-part"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The crossfader is a replaceable part, and that matters on a desk this long lived</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The crossfader is a
        {XONE_92.crossfaderModel}, fitted as standard on the Mk2, fully
        assignable from each music channel and user replaceable. Allen &amp;
        Heath's part number is {XONE_92.crossfaderPart}, and their wording is
        that it can be easily accessed should replacement be required.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> A mixer built around a filter that
        made its debut in 2004 outlives its own faders several times over. On a desk where
        the crossfader is not a serviceable part, wear ends the mixer's working
        life. Here it is a part number and an afternoon.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> If the crossfader feels gritty or cuts
        unevenly, order {XONE_92.crossfaderPart} rather than working around it,
        and keep one in the flight case if the desk is yours and touring. Allen
        &amp; Heath do not publish a lifespan or a cycle rating for it, so
        replacement is driven by feel rather than by a number.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Treating a worn crossfader as the
        end of the mixer, and assuming the channel faders are the same job: they
        are a different part and Allen &amp; Heath do not publish a number for
        them.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#crossfader-does-nothing">The crossfader does nothing</a> <a href="#revisions">Mk1, Mk2 and the Limited Edition</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="maintenance"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Keeping an analogue desk reliable over years of bookings</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Connectors, cables and the phono
        shorting plugs take the real wear on this mixer. There is no software
        state to reset, so what you are protecting is entirely physical.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> RCA and XLR connectors and the rear
        panel switches are what a touring life abuses, and a loose or bent pin
        causes the intermittent fault that is hardest to diagnose in a live
        room. Nothing about it will show up in a log, because there is no log.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Inspect cables before they go in the bag
        rather than when a fault appears, unplug by the connector and not the
        cable, keep the shorting plugs accounted for, and keep the desk covered
        in transport. The power supply is {XONE_92.mains}, so the same mixer
        travels between countries with nothing to switch and no fuse to
        change. It is {XONE_92.dimensions} and {XONE_92.weight}, and both of those come
        from Allen &amp; Heath's Limited Edition guide rather than from the Mk2
        specification sheet, which leaves them out.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Do not coil cables tightly around
        connectors, which is where strain damage starts, and do not coil audio
        cable in a loop beside a mains run.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#hum-and-ground-loops">Hum and ground loops</a> <a href="#crossfader-part">The crossfader is a replaceable part</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="sync" title="Compatibility" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="compatibility"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">What it works with, and what Allen &amp; Heath do not document</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Anything with a line or phono level
        output, on any of the four stereo music channels, plus microphones and
        line returns on the two MIC / RTN channels. There is no soundcard, no USB
        audio and no drivers, so nothing about a computer's operating system
        version affects this mixer.</p>
        <p><strong>Digital vinyl systems.</strong> Allen &amp; Heath do not
        document DVS or timecode for the Xone:92. No document of theirs for this
        model mentions timecode, Serato or Traktor, and there is no soundcard in
        it, so we say it is not documented rather than claiming it works or
        claiming it does not. DJs do run timecode through analogue mixers using a
        separate interface, and that arrangement is between you, your interface
        and your software, with nothing from Allen &amp; Heath to lean on if it
        misbehaves. Whatever is published for the Xone:96 is a different mixer
        with a different signal path and does not transfer.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> If you play on timecode, confirm your own
        interface and licence before the gig, and treat the mixer as four line
        inputs. If you play from players, this desk pairs with the era correct
        CD players below.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Turning up expecting to plug a
        laptop straight into the mixer. The only cable between a computer and
        this mixer is an audio cable from your own interface.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#midi">MIDI out</a> <a href="/knowledge/dictionary">The DJ dictionary</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="revisions"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Mk1, Mk2 and the Limited Edition</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> {XONE_92.revisions}. Allen &amp; Heath
        document the Mk1 and the Mk2 as distinct mixers with different internals
        and different manual page numbers, so a Mk1 in a venue is not simply an
        older looking Mk2. Allen &amp; Heath still list the Xone:92 in the Xone
        series and still publish its support documents, so both revisions are
        desks you will be handed rather than museum pieces.</p>
        <p><strong>The differences they list.</strong> It runs
        {XONE_92.faderType}. On a Mk1, changing that curve needs a hardware
        modification inside the mixer instead. Rack ears shipped with the Mk1 and do
        not ship with the Mk2. The Mk2 additions Allen &amp; Heath list are the
        mini innoFADER Pro crossfader, a storm grey colourway, that fader curve
        switch, improved channel input level and source matching, refined VCF
        tapers, optimised RIAA preamps, a removable dual ground adaptor and the
        rear panel mic clean feed switches. The Mk2 also claims redesigned
        analogue soft switching, so hot switching a channel into a filter does
        not put audible artefacts on the master.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters in a booth.</strong> Two of those changes are
        the difference between a fix you can perform and one you cannot: the
        fader curve, and the mic clean feed. If the mic has to reach the
        recording and the desk is a Mk1, the rear panel switch is not there to
        throw.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Reading a Mk2 page number or a Mk2
        rear panel diagram while standing at a Mk1. Check which one you have
        before you follow a diagram.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#mic-not-in-record">The mic is absent from the recording</a> <a href="#crossfader-part">The crossfader is a replaceable part</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="book" title="Frequently asked questions" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="xone92-acc" id="faq"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Quick answers</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <dl class="rb-errors">
          <dt>What firmware should it be on?</dt><dd>The question does not apply. Allen &amp; Heath publish no firmware, no drivers and no release notes for this mixer, and there is no USB audio to need any.</dd>
          <dt>Is it a four channel mixer or a six channel mixer?</dt><dd>Allen &amp; Heath say both. Their product page says {XONE_92.channelsProductPage}, their technical specification sheet says {XONE_92.channelsSpecSheet}. Physically: {XONE_92.channelsDetail}.</dd>
          <dt>Why is the mic missing from my recording?</dt><dd>By design. The mic inputs do not route to the BOOTH or RECORD outputs until the clean feed switches on the rear panel say otherwise.</dd>
          <dt>Does it do DVS?</dt><dd>Allen &amp; Heath do not document DVS or timecode for this mixer, and it has no soundcard. Anything that works does so through your own interface, with nothing published to fall back on.</dd>
          <dt>Can I use it as a soundcard for my laptop?</dt><dd>No. There is no USB audio on this mixer. {XONE_92.digitalIo}.</dd>
          <dt>Where is unity on the master?</dt><dd>Unity sits {XONE_92.masterUnityPosition}. Fully clockwise gives {XONE_92.masterAtMax}.</dd>
          <dt>Can I replace the crossfader myself?</dt><dd>Yes. It is a {XONE_92.crossfaderModel}, Allen &amp; Heath part {XONE_92.crossfaderPart}, and they describe it as easily accessed should replacement be required.</dd>
          <dt>Is there a service manual?</dt><dd>No. Allen &amp; Heath publish a block diagram instead, which is the closest thing to service documentation available for this mixer.</dd>
          <dt>Can I lift the mains earth to kill a hum?</dt><dd>No. Allen &amp; Heath call it a potentially lethal fault risk on a metal chassis mixer. Isolate the ground in the audio cabling instead.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </details>

